{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Surfaced by the 2026-08-05/06 codebase-health audit and scoped as its own epic rather than folded\ninto the janitor cleanup. Audit record: `.joe-janitor/report-2026-08-06.md`.\n\n## Why\n\nThe Jira reconciler's user-facing surface leaks internal mechanics and couples rebar core to GitHub\nActions. Six verbs today (`reconcile`, `jira-onboard`, `bridge-probe`, `bridge-status`,\n`bridge-fsck`, `purge-bridge`), of which two are dead: `bridge-status`'s producer has never existed\nin this repo (the reader was a knowing tombstone at extraction), and `purge-bridge` has five\ncommits, none of them feature or bug work, and no caller anywhere.\n\n`--mode`'s five values cannot be explained without internals because the enum FUSES two concepts —\n\"how much may this run change?\" and \"is the bridge allowed to run at all?\". Splitting them into\n`--max-changes` and `pause`/`resume` is what makes the rest of the surface writable in plain\nlanguage.\n\nrebar core shells out to `gh` for a safety precondition (`fsck_repair.py:278`), which is a\nportability violation: a client without GitHub Actions gets a destructive-repair guard that\nsilently does nothing. Meanwhile ~185 lines of logic live in the GHA workflow — including a\n106-line CAS push loop that re-implements `_store/push.py` and has diverged from it in both\ndirections.\n\n## Decisions already taken\n\n- Noun is **`bridge`** — provider-neutral (names the mechanism, not the vendor), zero collision,\n  near-zero migration cost, already the repo's word (`BRIDGE_ALERT`, `bridge_state/`).\n  `jira` was rejected because other trackers are planned.\n- **`bridge preview` / `bridge sync` become primary while `reconcile` remains a compatibility\n  adapter.** The defaults invert (`reconcile` means dry-run via a wrapper; `bridge sync` mutates),\n  so the adapter preserves the old dry-run default instead of naively aliasing to sync.\n- `bridge fsck` is kept; the earlier `check` suggestion is dropped.\n- **`status` is canonical.** The heartbeat canary consumes it on a schedule and escalates via\n  tickets, rather than being a parallel alarm.\n- Canonical bridge exit codes collapse to **0/1/2**; benign outcomes (busy, reschedule, paused)\n  exit 0 with a message. Legacy old-vocabulary adapters retain historical numeric translations\n  during the migration so older workflows and environments continue to work.\n- Flags survive only if their help text can be written without internals, and only if justified by\n  CI, local, or troubleshooting use. Ten candidates reduced to four operational flags (`--max-changes`, `--only`, `--except`, `--max-age`).\n- MCP and library get corresponding `bridge` surfaces — rebar's philosophy is three interfaces for\n  shared functionality.\n- Every Gerrit change preserves operational continuity: additive foundations and compatibility\n  adapters keep old vocabulary functional while every in-repo workflow, environment config,\n  script, test, and documented invocation migrates to the new primary vocabulary in the same\n  shippable change. A real legacy entrypoint is not removed in this epic merely because in-repo\n  references have migrated. Commands proven dead with no replacement or consumer may remain\n  removed with that evidence recorded. Continuity-coupled tickets may form a Gerrit relation\n  stack and submit together only after every patch is green.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `rebar bridge <verb>` is the canonical surface: setup, check-access, fsck, status, preview,\n      sync, pause, resume (`fsck` is settled — `doctor` was never a legacy command and is not shipped)\n- [x] Current workflows/docs use the canonical bridge surface; `reconcile` and `bridge-status`\n      remain functional compatibility entrypoints with their old safe defaults/contracts.\n      `purge-bridge` remains removed only because the audited repository and deployment inventory\n      prove no consumer and there is no replacement operation; release notes record all mappings.\n- [x] Every shipped flag's help text is written without internals and is justified by CI, local, or troubleshooting use\n- [x] Canonical bridge exits are 0/1/2 and the current CI whitelist block is deleted; legacy\n      command adapters retain historical codes for older workflow/environment continuity.\n- [x] Pause is remote-persisted, carries a reason, and cannot wedge a mixed-version fleet\n- [x] rebar core requires no `gh` binary for any documented operation\n- [x] **The bridge runs locally, on GitHub Actions, and on Jenkins** — verified per the portability AC below\n- [x] The 106-line YAML push loop is deleted and `_store/push.py` is reconciled with it\n- [x] `docs/user-guide.md:364`'s broken `--dry-run` example cannot recur\n\n## Rollback and blast radius\n\nThis epic makes changes that are hard to reverse on a LIVE scheduled job (the bridge runs every 20\nminutes on GHA), so each carries an explicit backout:\n\n- **Default inversion is the highest risk.** `rebar reconcile` with no arguments means DRY-RUN;\n  `bridge sync` with no arguments MUTATES. The compatibility adapter therefore preserves the old\n  dry-run default and maps explicit legacy modes into the same typed core request. It must never be\n  a naive alias to argument-less sync. Real-entrypoint parity tests prove stale callers remain safe.\n- **Backout for the surface change** is the revert of its own additive change; compatibility\n  adapters mean either the pre-change or post-change binary still accepts old vocabulary, and no\n  data migration is involved.\n- **The gate/pause format migration is the one change with a persistent artifact** and it is ordered\n  reader-ships-first (see child 1104): the reader tolerating both formats lands and is deployed BEFORE\n  any writer emits the new blob, so a rollback to the prior binary still reads the gate. The migration\n  is additive — `gated_mode` is retained — so no downgrade wedges the fleet.\n- **Deleting the 106-line YAML push loop** is guarded by first reconciling `_store/push.py` with it\n  (child 1c1b, which 2db2 depends on); the workflow change lands only after core provably covers the\n  divergences, and reverts independently.\n- **The `bridge fsck` output contract breaks once**, deliberately (child 030f decision 1), with a\n  CHANGELOG entry. The sole in-repo consumer reads `binding_drift` only.\n\n## Portability AC (verifiable in-session, not by a future adopter)\n\n- [x] A contract test runs the entrypoint under scrubbed environments for {local, github-actions, jenkins, gitlab, bare} and passes identically; it fails if any vendor variable is read or any `gh` invocation is required\n- [x] The same test asserts a full-depth clone succeeds and a **shallow clone is rejected with a named error** rather than proceeding (bug f193)\n- [x] `Jenkinsfile` and `.gitlab-ci.yml` exist as REAL COMMITTED FILES that the docs reference rather than duplicate\n- [x] The GHA artifact shipped as an example is the workflow rebar actually runs (or a reusable workflow both consume), so it is exercised every 20 minutes\n- [x] All three configs are asserted to share one exit-code table, one secret-name set, and one timeout, each cross-checked against the code rather than hardcoded\n- [x] The Jenkinsfile's `sh` body is asserted to be a SINGLE-QUOTED Groovy string (a double-quoted GString interpolates the secret before the shell sees it, defeating masking)\n- [x] `.gitlab-ci.yml` validates against GitLab's official JSON schema, offline. The VALIDATOR is\n      already a declared core dependency (`jsonschema`, see pyproject.toml), but the SCHEMA DOCUMENT\n      is not — it is VENDORED into the repo at a pinned version with its source URL and retrieval\n      date recorded, so the check needs no network and no new dependency\n- [x] shellcheck runs over script bodies extracted from all three configs, failing loudly on an\n      unparseable config. **Prior-art reconciliation (required):** `det_lint.py:183-190` records a\n      deliberate decision NOT to shell out to shellcheck, on two grounds — it is a heavy external\n      dependency, and rebar's verify commands are POLYGLOT. Only the first ground transfers here:\n      CI script bodies are bash by construction, so the polyglot objection does not apply and\n      shellcheck is the correct tool for this input. We therefore ADOPT shellcheck as an explicitly\n      declared test-harness prerequisite for this one check, NOT as a rebar runtime dependency, and\n      NOT by extending the bespoke lint. It stays absent from `pyproject.toml` runtime deps.\n- [x] The shellcheck prerequisite is PROVISIONED, not assumed: CI installs a pinned version, and the\n      test FAILS with a named, actionable error when the binary is missing — it must never silently\n      skip, which would void the \"verifiable in-session\" guarantee this AC block claims\n- [x] Uncovered risks are documented as KNOWN GAPS outside the AC, not as future adopter work"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786431699871375001,"uuid":"8d9c6f0e-e6c8-4e7a-a160-381b13bc9e35"}